{"sdk_http_response":{"headers":{"x-gemini-service-tier":"standard","content-type":"application/json; charset=UTF-8","vary":"Origin, X-Origin, Referer","content-encoding":"gzip","date":"Fri, 27 Nov 2026 17:13:23 GMT","server":"scaffolding on HTTPServer2","x-xss-protection":"0","x-frame-options":"SAMEORIGIN","x-content-type-options":"nosniff","server-timing":"gfet4t7; dur=32454","alt-svc":"h3=\":443\"; ma=2592000,h3-29=\":443\"; ma=2592000","transfer-encoding":"chunked"},"body":null},"candidates":[{"content":{"parts":[{"media_resolution":null,"code_execution_result":null,"executable_code":null,"file_data":null,"function_call":null,"function_response":null,"inline_data":null,"text":"**Claim Evaluation: A Deep Dive**\n\nOkay, here's my assessment of these claims. I've gone through each one methodically, applying my understanding of category theory, homotopy theory, and the relevant literature to ensure accurate grading.\n\nFirst, I received the prompt to assess the 20 claims based on the text provided. My process involved carefully checking each claim against the text and the rubric, ensuring that each statement extracted from the text aligns with the given criteria, subject, relation, and object. I've double-checked locators for accuracy and confirmed the claimed relations based on the available information.\n\n*   **Claim 1:** Theorem 4.7.8 indeed states the commutativity of the diagram. The quote and the grade, *faithful*, are confirmed.\n*   **Claim 2:** This one was a bit more involved. The \"depends-on\" relation needed careful consideration. I verified that the \"internal Yoneda lemma\" is indeed presented as a result and that it depends on the \"internal Grothendieck construction.\" I confirmed the locator, considering both \"Main results\" and \"Related work\" sections to determine the most precise reference. The rubric suggests that the single string used for \"object\" in a binary relation is a valid approach. It passes the test for relation validity and the locator is acceptable, so I gave it a *faithful* grade.\n*   **Claim 3:** The equivalence between \"monomorphism\" and \"map whose diagonal is an equivalence\" is explicitly stated in Example 2.5.9, so *faithful*.\n*   **Claim 4:** The equivalence between `Cat(B)` and `Shv_{Cat_∞}(B)` is a main result from Section 1, so the claim is *faithful*.\n*   **Claim 5:** Theorem 4.5.1 clearly states the canonical equivalence between \\[C,Ω] and LFib_C. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 6:** The definition of \"LFib_C\" as a large B-category whose objects are left fibrations is stated in Section 1. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 7:** The equivalence between the composite functor and the identity functor on B_Δ is precisely stated, *faithful*.\n*   **Claim 8:**  The equivalence between \"S-category\" and \"complete Segal space\" is presented, and confirmed. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 9:** The definition of \"saturated class of maps\" is based on the conditions of Definition 2.5.5, which is presented. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 10:** The use of \"left properness\" to present \"homotopy pushouts\" is clearly stated. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 11:** The proposition from 2.5.2 clearly has \"L and R are stable under retracts\", *faithful*.\n*   **Claim 12:** Proposition 3.2.9 shows that \"Cat(B) is an accessible localisation of B_Δ, hence presentable\", *faithful*.\n*   **Claim 13:** The equivalence from Proposition 3.2.7 of C is a B-category based on the relations of Segal maps. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 14:** The definition of \"strong epimorphism\" aligns with the given statement. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 15:** The ∞-category of complete Segal spaces as a model of ∞-categories is confirmed. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 16:** The factorization system and the citation from Lurie is exactly as stated. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 17:**  Strong epimorphisms and covers are clearly equivalent, *faithful*.\n*   **Claim 18:** Here, I needed to check carefully for the exact wording to justify the \"generalises\" relation. I concluded that it is valid as fixing a parameter of a B-category implies a specialisation (the S-category) and therefore the generalisation holds. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 19:**  I verified that the parametrised infinity-categories are a special case of a larger class of categories, \"categories in an arbitrary ∞-topos.\" The prompt requires the use of one of the defined relationships, and so I confirmed it here. *Faithful*.\n*   **Claim 20:** The correspondence, which implies equivalence, between parametrized ∞-categories and categories in presheaf ∞-topoi is confirmed. *Faithful*.\n\nIn each case, I've tried to be as precise as possible, ensuring that the claims are faithful representations of the original text. I have considered each relation carefully and verified the quoted text as a representation of that relation.\n\n\n","thought":true,"thought_signature":null,"video_metadata":null,"tool_call":null,"tool_response":null,"part_metadata":null,"audio_transcription":null,"media_processing":null},{"media_resolution":null,"code_execution_result":null,"executable_code":null,"file_data":null,"function_call":null,"function_response":null,"inline_data":null,"text":"{\"n\": 1, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Theorem 4.7.8. There is a commutative diagram ... of (large) ℬ-categories.\"}\n{\"n\": 2, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"By making use of the Grothendieck construction, we derive our second main result, an internal version of Yoneda’s lemma.\"}\n{\"n\": 3, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"By construction, a map in 𝒞 is a monomorphism if and only if its diagonal is an equivalence.\"}\n{\"n\": 4, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"one obtains an equivalence Cat(ℬ) ≃ Shv_{Cat_∞}(ℬ) between the ∞-category of ℬ-categories and the ∞-category of Cat_∞-valued sheaves on ℬ.\"}\n{\"n\": 5, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"There is a canonical equivalence [𝖢,Ω] ≃ 𝖫𝖥𝗂𝖻_𝖢 that is natural in 𝖢.\"}\n{\"n\": 6, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"we define a large ℬ-category 𝖫𝖥𝗂𝖻_𝖢 whose objects are given by left fibrations over 𝖢.\"}\n{\"n\": 7, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The composite functor ... is equivalent to the identity functor on ℬ_Δ.\"}\n{\"n\": 8, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 3.2.7 shows that an 𝒮-category is precisely a complete Segal space as developed by Rezk [Rez01].\"}\n{\"n\": 9, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Then S is saturated if (1) S contains all equivalences in 𝒞 and is closed under composition; (2) S is closed under small colimits in Fun(Δ^1,𝒞); (3) S is closed under pushouts.\"}\n{\"n\": 10, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"As the Quillen model structure on the 1-category of simplicial sets is left proper, ordinary pushouts along monomorphisms of simplicial sets present homotopy pushouts in 𝒮.\"}\n{\"n\": 11, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"both ℛ and ℒ are stable under taking retracts.\"}\n{\"n\": 12, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The inclusion Cat(ℬ) ↪ ℬ_Δ exhibits Cat(ℬ) as an accessible localisation of ℬ_Δ.\"}\n{\"n\": 13, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"The following conditions are equivalent: (1) C is a ℬ-category; (2) for all n ≥ 2 the maps C_n → C_1 ×_{C_0} ⋯ ×_{C_0} C_1 as well as the map C_0 → (C_0 × C_0) ×_{C_1 × C_1} C_3 are equivalences.\"}\n{\"n\": 14, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"we say that a map is a strong epimorphism if it is internally left orthogonal to every monomorphism.\"}\n{\"n\": 15, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"By a theorem of Joyal and Tierney [JT07] the ∞-category of complete Segal spaces is a model for the ∞-category of ∞-categories Cat_∞\"}\n{\"n\": 16, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Let 𝒞 be a presentable ∞-category and let S be a small set of maps in 𝒞. Then there is a factorisation system (ℒ,ℛ) in 𝒞 with ℛ = S^⊥ and ℒ = ^⊥ℛ.\"}\n{\"n\": 17, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"If 𝒞 is an ∞-topos, then strong epimorphisms are precisely covers, i.e. effective epimorphisms in the terminology of [Lur09a].\"}\n{\"n\": 18, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Proposition 3.2.7 shows that an 𝒮-category is precisely a complete Segal space as developed by Rezk [Rez01].\"}\n{\"n\": 19, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"Such parametrised ∞-categories are a special case of the more general notion of categories in an arbitrary ∞-topos\"}\n{\"n\": 20, \"grade\": \"faithful\", \"reason\": \"they precisely correspond to categories in presheaf ∞-topoi.\"}\nGRADES-END","thought":null,"thought_signature":"EuFsCt5sAVjVB3uduiFn-8O1kBPZ5sQdqR9qmjbLFnDE89rmsPa7LaTm27FnqhxNgV3JYnwHG0WXntB8GK1OG8h3X4EhktIR-e8edtx8EiXOZsMvQ3f9-D57k3FZ4CAPL6civ4aDomdb0-UjhVWUTJ8CFEE2c-QzB-Q8umoyhIdneGm-qMAw_W7mIFhHGIqc6yEYCA64oeVYFB8ii6Gzpg6UidheryEpjw0kS65DpEV-6zF7Z7w2I6cdIzRn0v5vL1O7Qm-YkED29eIb5IuwW4UxbRc0cEW41l1n-iAG2BPGNEm3dRnrBOCCnlh3wTQgdFW3cFnIkA1BsKMZiygXg_P06ZFgYoccV66wj2udgAqjteUg33imKpngld70whpKHCkAT9OczuJC9_ba0iflg_Ndn4vdqOdn_Jp7R7AuPOoZW4DaRAO9DEkgLhTQb34YYJRsnoiO7RokXg2tvaNhWMd0wZQFlGhr15W1ztTZKFyyLufp2fb_eWu1Ds12iULn3Gc1fVUBAPNSacJqyUzSfWOgGNzyA3zOAWnyKB_NaSxrGewDf0NXSz1qnbI33ZbwoahykstVz0Mx7C0Rdg97anGqBVB9-UafT_LeyoCFYSfnRNfO3PEyaw0M6WcDluXGE-IuZdEE6LQ5_ovSwXq6NirZltflgWfbC4tcL7Q3gVCYPsIPu759oOHZo5_VNcjvlBFtgOh-nzZZldFa_5mAsNHxqOq2EtwFM4PTEkPPIxCoXGYs07KEIx-YPQM6QUhsaErMneUuQ39DESH0RtLOghLbHBWCP6W3GnVNl_x9JtFJKDNi6f2vLOOOWpkZGdaoUopzrn1WS9PT-Klq-0z-TSZWgMGZtNLbSVCH2UF80zQvmi2ACm-QLh6VqLO4YW8j2-cnhIHW5SldT-NwLXDoQddpuH3Kl0kgSDIJjceK80MuBgAwIzR0JsPNP0leakds0Wb6zr3N2RnwMhG1LYUmnQupuwaAoyJK4E2kYU7ryeONiYPzParNsQQcptUXX7MAxJCmBdPBHBh6YcYhQLaqUpAq3yiv_y3QAWJFwNipQpDjGo4K26cUDZcQvP4gtZ5CwpL84dw2clJm-r5fvaH3CSZ8EdE1Ve1MB1zEQpgB82eRLSSVjNAeUc9i2-pDxOsFrfCKEW2mkJyZSm966bCx8sUGT4cHwni2cX9SNCXYcpDBOuesufq3iDgshXTNg-pa-m0GDJ0hXkASj2aga8G6ywtWMKA83elr8ctnW1NaSswgEl7MtWHd80jmCZ_hbVKvMF_-UwQkmJPa5aEfgHgNI2wwoUaN8dXBOhbqI58tJ62Ph13uHTV7ikh9Acqsn4fpKw9120GPTR1Yc94lWf2TxcaikN04Bx-HvA_RPEj5a_vYQCj8JRW2Vtd2SoPc0SDxAbzjMF8TI1EjF1kP3IiaZKmV8SxW-zA